  "example_content": [
    {
      "text": "The media framing issue is real and measurable. Content analyses have shown that headlines disproportionately use active voice for Israeli actions ('Israel strikes') and passive voice for Hamas actions ('rockets launched'). Casualty figures from the Hamas-run Gaza Health Ministry are reported without consistent caveats, while IDF casualty claims are routinely flagged as 'unverified.' Whether intentional or not, this creates an asymmetry in how audiences assign responsibility.",
      "platform": "reddit",
      "confidence": 0.89,
      "is_influencer_framing": false
    },
    {
      "text": "I work in media. The coverage asymmetry has structural causes, not just bias. Gaza is small and devastated \u2014 every strike is visible and photogenic. Hamas operates underground and doesn't allow independent reporting. The visual economy of the conflict inherently favors one narrative. That said, outlets should do more to contextualize the tunnel network, rocket fire, and Hamas's strategy of maximizing civilian exposure.",
      "platform": "reddit",
      "confidence": 0.81,
      "is_influencer_framing": false
    },
